00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
we're going to begin with this president  biden is preparing to mark 100 days in  office this week with a variety of  events including  his first speech to a joint session of  congress and a drive-in rally in georgia  a new cbs news poll finds 65 percent of  americans approve of how he's handling  this pandemic  the president set several goals when he  entered the white house including  requiring mass where he is legally able  to like at federal buildings  and during interstate travel while masks  are still required in those areas  some states have taken a very different  approach in fact at least  13 states have lifted their mass mandate  orders  only 26 states now require you to wear a  mask  in public the president also said an  initial goal of 100 million vaccine  doses administered in his first 100 days  in office  before increasing that goal to 200  million and that number was achieved  last week we are now at nearly  229 million doses administered as for  who has gotten at least one dose  64 percent are white 12 percent are  hispanic  more than 8 percent are black and more  than 5 percent are  asian and a new cbs news poll finds  22 percent of americans say they will  not get the vaccine  at all new data from the kaiser  foundation suggests  vaccine supply will likely outweigh  demand in the coming weeks  dr ashish shah the dean of brown  university school of public health joins  us now dr john good morning to you  good morning thanks for having me back  we always appreciate you being on we  have a new survey out a new poll from  cbs  showing that about a quarter to a fifth  to a quarter of americans say they will  not  get a vaccine of any kind that is a big  improvement in fact from february the  number is getting smaller  is it small enough  yeah i think it will get smaller yet i'm  hopeful and part of it is a lot of those  people  i think are still waiting and seeing and  if that number diminishes further  then it will be good enough we don't  need to have every american vaccinated  but we do need to have  most and i hope i hope most americans  decide this is really a safe and  effective vaccine that  is good for them it's good for their  families we've also learned from the cdc  that about 5 million americans have not  returned to get their second vaccination  so one of the moderna  or pfizer shots is one shot good enough  does that provide any  protection for people yeah one shot is  definitely not good enough  it's very very clear from the data that  people need both shots  and so what i say to people is if you  miss your second shot don't worry about  it go back and get it now  there's no problem in in having it been  delayed a little bit  but it is critical that everybody get  their second shot because  that's the only way we know that it's  going to really protect you for  any extended period of time dr xia you  wrote in an  op-ed recently that the u.s has  strategic interests  to help india manage uh the coronavirus  crisis there which is escalating in  alarming proportions right now  what more should the u.s do do you think  yeah i think the u.s can do a lot and  even since i've written that op-ed the  president and  the federal government has come out in  strong support of acting i think it's  fabulous we should be helping them with  their hospital supplies with  testing and also helping them ramp up  vaccinations you know we have  so many vaccines right now that i think  we should be sending excess supply  especially of vaccines that we're not  using here  to india to help india out at this  moment  you know i think it's important to to  emphasize how important it is to help  other countries when the  coronavirus first started we we were  told  if you if you haven't been to china  don't know anybody who's come from china  you don't really need to worry about it  and we see how quickly  this has spread so i i think it's  important emphasize  what happens in other parts of the world  really does affect us eventually don't  you think  yeah absolutely this is a global  pandemic we know for instance that when  you see new variants arise  anywhere it ends up on our shores very  quickly  we are really all in this together and  if we're going to get through this  pandemic  we've all got to help each other bring  the pandemic under control the cdc says  they're thinking about  adjusting the guidelines for masks that  we may not have to wear them outside  where do you stand on that do you think  it's time to rethink how  and where and when we wear our mask  you know absolutely gail we we have  learned over the last year that there's  relatively little spread outdoors  especially if you're not packed together  in large groups  so i expect the cdc to come out with new  guidance on this and i expect a lot of  states  to begin to peel back outdoor mask  mandates i think  that's probably pretty reasonable we've  got to keep indoor mass mandates  in for a little bit longer that is where  most of the spread is happening but  uh pulling back on outdoor stuff does  seem pretty reasonable at this moment  all right dr jaw and the other uh thing  in the in the near future is the fall  start of school once again do you expect  children to be  available for vaccinations at that point  well first of all i think all kids  should be back in school this fall and i  think they will be  teachers have all been vaccinated  schools are really a safe place for kids  to go back i expect  older kids to be vaccinated by the fall  i think  young kids under 12 probably not but  i i don't think that should slow us down  at all from getting everybody back in  school this fall  all right well the possibility of not  having to wear a mask outdoors during  the summer and not getting a masked tan  i like that possibility dr shisha  thank you very much we appreciate it  thank you

